  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>The Nartuby runs through Draguignan, Trans-en-Provence and La Motte before joining the Argens, offering several waterfalls. A Himalayan footbridge, installed in 2021 (Var Tourisme, Dracénie Provence Verdon), spans the gorges at Trans-en-Provence, as part of the "Boucle panoramique du Calant": 1.6 km, about 40 minutes, easy with no elevation gain according to the town hall, starting from the village centre.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>Unlike the big Verdon lakes, the mood here is more intimate: the town hall describes the Nartuby as a torrential river carving gorges through the heart of the village. Neither the town nor the tourist office publishes any rule or swimming area for dogs: think of this place as a walk, not a swimming spot.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: the suspension bridge gives an impressive view of the water below — be careful if your dog isn't keen on heights.</div>

      <h2>The Calant loop in practice</h2>
      <p>According to Dracénie Provence Verdon Tourisme, the start is opposite the pharmacy: take the stairs, then follow the Nartuby, passing under the Pont Vieux. The town hall and Var Tourisme also give as a starting point the boules ground opposite the town hall. The route is marked by nine connected information panels (NFC chips or QR codes) on the church, the fountains, the chapels, the riverside woodland, the geology of the gorges, the building of the footbridge, the mills and the hydroelectric plant. Markings on the ground guide the loop.</p>
      <p>The Himalayan footbridge is 70 m long and spans the gorges 30 m above. Installed in 2021, it is open to all, free of charge, all year round, every day (Var Tourisme). At the end of the footbridge, a small path to the right follows the river to the Pont de la Motte, then you return to the village by the path on the left and the place du Souvenir français. A map of the village car parks is available on the town hall website; for any question, the tourist office answers on 04 98 10 51 05.</p>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The town hall also offers the family walk "Le Puits aérien": 5 km, 1h40, easy, 100 m of elevation gain, leading to a work by the Belgian engineer Achille Knapen. In the village centre, place des Moulins, below the town hall, overlooks the ruins of old oil mills whose wheels were driven by the Nartuby.</p>
    </div>

